    "text": "WorldDirector's persistent dynamic memory explicitly stores object attributes (position, velocity, orientation) that persist across time steps, enabling state inference without requiring visual observation at every frame.",
    "topic": "Persistent memory function"
  },
  {
    "text": "The phrase 'without continuous visual observation' means that the simulator does not need dense, per-step visual input; it can rely on memory when objects are temporarily unobserved, but initial and occasional visual observations are still required to seed and correct the memory.",
    "topic": "Visual observation requirement"
  },
  {
    "text": "WorldDirector is a memory-augmented world simulator designed to reduce dependence on constant visual sensing; it does not claim to operate entirely without any visual input.",
    "topic": "Design goal clarification"
  },
  {
    "text": "The persistent dynamic memory works alongside visual observations: when visual data is available, it can update the memory; when missing, the memory provides continuity, thus enabling 'controllable' simulation without drift.",
    "topic": "Memory–observation interplay"
  }
]
